conversation, or the session was launched with `csandbox --worktree` (the
system prompt says so explicitly when it was).

WITH --worktree: first check whether you're ALREADY in one - if your current
directory's path contains `/.claude/worktrees/`, work where you are; calling
EnterWorktree again nests a worktree one level deeper, and repeated across
sessions that once produced 267GB of nested node_modules. Otherwise call
`EnterWorktree` before touching any files, so edits land on a disposable
branch off the current commit (`worktree.baseRef: head`; committed history
only, not the user's uncommitted changes). When done, leave the branch
(`ExitWorktree` with `action: "keep"`) for the user to review and merge -
don't push, open a PR, or merge it yourself unless asked.

Any worktree you DO create by hand (e.g. "check out branch X in a worktree")
